    explanation: "The ideal gas law is the master equation of gas behaviour, and this ideal gas law calculator uses it to find how many moles of gas fill a container from the pressure, volume, and temperature you measure. It applies the constant R equal to 0.082057 litre-atmospheres per mole-kelvin, so with pressure in atmospheres, volume in litres, and temperature in kelvin, the answer comes out in moles.\n\nThe ideal gas law matters because it links four quantities that are otherwise hard to relate. Given any three, you can find the fourth, which is why chemists use it to work out reaction yields, engineers use it to size pressure vessels, and meteorologists use it to model the atmosphere. Rearranged as n = PV divided by RT, it turns easily measured bulk properties into the amount of substance present, a figure you cannot read off a gauge directly.\n\nThe law works because an ideal gas is treated as countless tiny particles that neither attract nor repel and occupy negligible volume. Under everyday conditions of moderate pressure and temperature well above the boiling point, real gases follow it closely. A good ideal gas law calculator therefore gives accurate results for classroom problems and many practical situations, only losing accuracy at very high pressures or very low temperatures where gases start to condense.",
